Executive Briefing: Analytic Hierarchies of the von Mangoldt Function, Stieltjes Constants, and Explicit Formulas

1. Executive Summary

This briefing document synthesizes key research on the analytic hierarchies of the von Mangoldt function \Lambda_k(n), the zero-residue polynomials P_k(\rho, \lambda), the Stieltjes constants \gamma_m, and their extensions to generalized L-functions and modular forms.

The central framework bridges local analytic data at the pole s=1 of the Riemann zeta function \zeta(s) with global arithmetic fluctuations in prime distributions. By applying shift operators and logarithmic differentiation to Dirichlet series, the classical von Mangoldt function \Lambda(n) generalizes into a hierarchy of functions \Lambda_k(n) = \Lambda(n)(\log n)^k. The partial sums \Psi_k(x) = \sum_{n \le x} \Lambda_k(n) are expressed precisely via explicit formulas containing smooth main terms M_k(x) governed by the Stieltjes constants \gamma_m, alongside oscillatory terms driven by the non-trivial zeros \rho of \zeta(s).

Key Analytical Takeaways

* Duality of Local and Global Structures: The Stieltjes constants \gamma_m encode local cumulant-like expansion data of \zeta(s) at s=1, while \Lambda_k(n) describes higher-order moments/fluctuations of primes. Contour integration and Mellin inversion map local Taylor coefficients at s=1 into global polynomial main terms, while non-trivial zeros translate into oscillatory error terms.
* Universal Zero-Residue Polynomials (P_k): At simple zeros \rho, the residue terms in the Mellin inversion resolve into zero-residue polynomials P_k(\rho, \lambda) (where \lambda = \log x). These polynomials satisfy an exponential generating function (EGF), a two-term linear recurrence relation, Appell lowering (\partial_\lambda P_k = k P_{k-1}), and an interdependence differential equation (\partial_\lambda [x^\rho P_k] = -x^\rho \lambda^k).
* Critical Line Scaling and Symmetry: When zero pairs (\rho, 1-\rho) are combined, the oscillatory terms factor into an x^{1/2} base amplitude modulated by hyperbolic weights x^{\pm(\beta - 1/2)}. Under the Riemann Hypothesis (\beta = 1/2), these hyperbolic weights collapse to 1, yielding pure x^{1/2} oscillations modulated by P_k(\rho, \lambda).
* Exact Zero Power Sums: For k \ge 2, the Stieltjes constants \gamma_k are directly linked to the symmetric power sums of the non-trivial zeros S_k = \sum_\rho (1-\rho)^{-k} via the closed formula: \gamma_k = (k-1)! \left[ 1 - (1 - 2^{-k})\zeta(k) - S_k \right]
* Algebraic-Combinatorial Unification: Bell polynomials explicitly mirror prime factorizations, providing an algebraic exponential model for multiplicative functions under Dirichlet convolution. Parallelly, the Hasse-Stirling operator framework and Gauss's Digamma Theorem connect prime logarithmic distributions to transcendental value classes and finite Fourier expansions.

2. Foundations of the von Mangoldt Hierarchies

2.1 The Classical von Mangoldt Function

The classical von Mangoldt function \Lambda(n) weights integers by their prime power structure: \Lambda(n) = \begin{cases} \log p, & \text{if } n = p^k \text{ for a prime } p \text{ and integer } k \ge 1, \\ 0, & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}

For \Re(s) > 1, its Dirichlet generating series corresponds to the negative logarithmic derivative of the Riemann zeta function: \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\Lambda(n)}{n^s} = -\frac{\zeta'(s)}{\zeta(s)} The logarithmic derivative acts as an analytic dictionary: the simple pole of \zeta(s) at s=1 dictates the average density of primes, while the non-trivial zeros \rho induce oscillations in prime counts.

2.2 Higher-Order Hierarchies via Derivative Shifts

For any integer k \ge 0, the k-th von Mangoldt function \Lambda_k(n) is defined via its Dirichlet series: \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\Lambda_k(n)}{n^s} = (-1)^k \frac{d^k}{ds^k} \left[ -\frac{\zeta'(s)}{\zeta(s)} \right], \quad \Re(s) > 1

Evaluating the derivatives yields explicit arithmetic definitions: \Lambda_k(n) = \Lambda(n)(\log n)^k \Lambda_k(p^r) = (\log p)^{k+1} r^k \quad \text{for prime powers } n = p^r

The Shift Operator Mechanism

Applying a shift s \mapsto s-t generates an exponential generating function (EGF) in t: F(s,t) := -\frac{\zeta'(s-t)}{\zeta(s-t)} = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\Lambda(n)}{n^{s-t}} = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\Lambda(n) n^t}{n^s} Expanding n^t = e^{t \log n} = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{t^k (\log n)^k}{k!} gives: F(s,t) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{t^k}{k!} \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{\Lambda(n)(\log n)^k}{n^s} = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{t^k}{k!} \left[ (-1)^k \frac{d^k}{ds^k} \left(-\frac{\zeta'(s)}{\zeta(s)}\right) \right] Differentiating k times with respect to s inserts the logarithmic factor (-\log n)^k, generating higher logarithmic moments of the prime distribution.

